{"name":"Blue Elemental","hex":"#5588ee","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"elemental","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of, relating to, or being an element (as opposed to a compound).","Basic, fundamental or elementary."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A creature (usually a spirit) that is attuned with, or composed of, one of the classical elements: air, earth, fire and water or variations of them like ice, lightning, etc. They sometimes have unique proper names and sometimes are referred to as Air, Earth, Fire, or Water."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/elemental"}],"entries":[{"title":"Elemental","blocks":[{"text":"An elemental is a mythic supernatural being that is described in occult and alchemical works from around the time of the European Renaissance, and particularly elaborated in the 16th century works of Paracelsus. According to Paracelsus and his subsequent followers, there are four categories of elementals, which are gnomes, undines, sylphs, and salamanders. These correspond to the four Empedoclean elements of antiquity: earth, water, air, and fire, respectively. Terms employed for beings associated with alchemical elements vary by source and gloss."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Undine_Rising_from_the_Waters%2C_front.JPG/330px-Undine_Rising_from_the_Waters%2C_front.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elemental","wikidata":"Q1786347"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/The_Elements%2C_Chelsea_Porcelain_Manufactory_-_Indianapolis_Museum_of_Art_-_DSC00573.JPG/500px-The_Elements%2C_Chelsea_Porcelain_Manufactory_-_Indianapolis_Museum_of_Art_-_DSC00573.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/The_Elements%2C_Chelsea_Porcelain_Manufactory_-_Indianapolis_Museum_of_Art_-_DSC00573.JPG/1280px-The_Elements%2C_Chelsea_Porcelain_Manufactory_-_Indianapolis_Museum_of_Art_-_DSC00573.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/The_Elements%2C_Chelsea_Porcelain_Manufactory_-_Indianapolis_Museum_of_Art_-_DSC00573.JPG/500px-The_Elements%2C_Chelsea_Porcelain_Manufactory_-_Indianapolis_Museum_of_Art_-_DSC00573.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Rococo set of personification figurines of the Four Elements, 1760s, Chelsea porcelain","link":"https://